window.docContents[237].resolve({"/Automation/#Logical-Foundations--Automation___-More-Automation--Case-Study___-Regular-Expressions--The-_LPAR_Strong_RPAR_-Pumping-Lemma":{"contents":"Now here is the usual version of the pumping lemma. In addition to\nrequiring that s₂ ≠ [], it also strengthens the result to\ninclude the claim that s₁.length + s₂.length ≤ re.pumpingConstant.theorem pumping {α : Type} {re : RegExp α} {s : List α}\n    (hmatch : s =~ re) (hlen : re.pumpingConstant ≤ s.length) :\n    ∃ s₁ s₂ s₃ : List α,\n      s = s₁ ++ s₂ ++ s₃ ∧ s₂ ≠ [] ∧\n      s₁.length + s₂.length ≤ re.pumpingConstant ∧\n      ∀ m, s₁ ++ napp m s₂ ++ s₃ =~ re := by\n  sorry\n\n\n\nend RegExp\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Automation: More Automation\u0009Case Study: Regular Expressions","header":"10.5.5. The (Strong) Pumping Lemma","id":"/Automation/#Logical-Foundations--Automation___-More-Automation--Case-Study___-Regular-Expressions--The-_LPAR_Strong_RPAR_-Pumping-Lemma"},"/Preface/#Preface":{"contents":"The SF course at Penn (CIS 5000) sometimes attracts\nstudents who don't have enough math background and begin really\nflailing around the middle of the semester. I wonder if we could\nhelp these people weed themselves out by offering some kind of more\ndetailed self-assessment near the beginning, maybe in this\nchapter.Some concepts that I would hope people have seen before:* recursive, polymorphic functional programming over lists* abstract definitions involving relations (e.g., reflexive,\n    symmetric, transitive closure of a relation)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations","header":"1. Preface","id":"/Preface/#Preface"}});